- TODO: statistical review of Chapter 5 - Confidence intervals
    - Date: 2026-04-30
    - Summary
        - Two rounds of critic/defender on chapter.log, chapter.md, generate.py, notebook.ipynb.
        - Numerical interval values reproduce from code (e.g. Wald at 6/10 is 0.6 +/- 1.96 * sqrt(0.6*0.4/10) = [0.296, 0.904], rounds to the quoted [0.30, 0.90]).
        - Loop D figure shows N=20 only, but the narrative talks about N=10 and N=5 falling below nominal. The text is right about the phenomenon but the figure does not show it.
        - The chapter never states the single most-important interpretation point in the field: a frequentist CI is a property of the procedure, not a probability statement about the parameter, while a Bayesian credible interval is a probability statement about the parameter under the prior. The two-lens framing demands this be named.
        - Coverage at p = 0.5 is the most forgiving point for Wald. Wald's coverage oscillates badly as p varies (Brown, Cai, DasGupta 2001). A coverage-vs-p sweep at fixed N is needed to honestly show where Wald lies.
        - Bootstrap percentile is presented as a benign fallback. The known caveat (percentile bootstrap is biased on skewed sampling distributions and near boundaries, BCa exists exactly for this) is not mentioned.
    - Items
        - HIGH [APPLIED 2026-04-29]: state the interpretation difference between frequentist CI and Bayesian credible interval
            - Add a short callout (3 to 5 sentences) at the top of Loop A or as a separate "What does 95% mean?" subsection.
            - Frequentist 95% CI: under repeated experiments, 95% of the constructed intervals would contain the true p. The interval [0.30, 0.90] does NOT mean "p is in [0.30, 0.90] with probability 0.95". p is fixed; the interval is the random object.
            - Bayesian 95% credible interval: given the prior and the data, p falls in [0.32, 0.81] with posterior probability 0.95. This IS a probability statement about p.
            - Tie this back to chapter.log Loop A and chapter.md Loop A.
